d as follows  Section Code   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Entire tan white fibrous area  upper outer quadrant   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Central breast   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Inner breast   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Upper outer quadrant tissue that may represent possible nodes  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 5 5cm left mastectomy specimen with a suture designating the lateral margin  The overlying tan brown skin measures REMOVED_DATE x 15 0cm and contains a 3 0cm curved scar between the 12 and 3 o clock position that lies 2 5cm from the nipple  A second 4 5cm linear scar is present at 4 o clock  5 0cm from the nipple  The overlying REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm areola and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm nipple are grossly unremarkable  The specimen is inked as follows  anterior blue  posterior black  The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal two tan white  fibrous  stellate shaped scars  The first scar measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 5cm and lies in the central region approximately beneath the curved scar present between the 12 and 3 o clock position on the overlying skin  It lies 2 0cm from the overlying skin and 5 5cm from the posterior inked surface  The second scar measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm and lies in the lower outer quadrant beneath the linear scar at the 4 o clock position and lies 1 5cm from the overlying skin and 6 0cm from the posterior inked surface  The rest of the breast parenchyma shows diffuse tan white fibrous tissue and small cystic spaces filled with blue fluid  Representative sections are submitted as follows   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Entire first scar located in the central breast   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Posterior margin closest to the first scar   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Entire second stellate scar located in the lower outer quadrant   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Posterior margin closest to the second scar   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Upper outer quadrant   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Upper inner quadrant   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Upper outer quadrant that may represent lymph nodes  Final Diagnosis by Melinda J Lerwill M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE PART   BREAST  RIGHT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMYBREAST  LEFT   SIMPLE MASTE , Poor_(grade_III).
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Corrected Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  29 REMOVED_MONTH 96 ACCESSIONED ON  29 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 12 29 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2  REMOVED_DATE CM INVADING LYMPHATIC VESSELS DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  RADIATION CHANGES  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Blood vessel invasion is not seen  The margins of the specimen appear free of the tumor  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  06 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 14 55                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR ESTROGEN RECEPTOR PROTEIN  THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN  SEE NOTE  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that fai